# systemd-oomd policy for the pi worker slice. POLICY ONLY — no memory bounds
# live here; those are per-worker MemoryMax on pi-issue@<repo>-<N>.service
# (fleet-ops + 0509 MemoryMax=4G with NO MemoryHigh per fleet-ops#3930; the
# pi-issue@.service template keeps MemoryHigh=3G/MemoryMax=6G as the
# no-table fallback; heavy manager workers 3G/2G).
#
# Killing is the EXCEPTION, not the norm (Nish, 2026-08-26). Three layers, in
# order, and oomd is the last:
#   1. MIN_FREE_RAM_MB=2500 launch floor  -> ~13.1 GiB soft cap. PREVENTS work
#      starting. This is the brake that should do essentially all the work.
#   2. per-worker MemoryMax=4G            -> BOUNDS one worker. fleet-ops#3930
#      DROPPED the MemoryHigh throttle band for fleet-ops + 0509: MemoryHigh
#      throttling is what made oomd pressure-kill a random sibling (6
#      pi-issue@* kills in 1h on a 15 GB box; pi-issue@0509-1752 killed at a
#      94.3M peak — victim was not the offender). Without the band a worker
#      that exceeds 4G is OOM-killed locally at the cap and oomd has no
#      throttle-to-kill path on the worker slice. No process dies from
#      throttling; only a genuine 4G overflow dies, locally.
#   3. this                                -> KILLS, only when 1 and 2 have both
#      failed and the host is genuinely in trouble.
#
# Threshold set from measurement, not the default. Baseline memory pressure on
# this box: /proc/pressure/memory `some avg10` = 0.00 across 5 samples with the
# fleet running. 80% sustained for a full minute is therefore nowhere near
# normal operation - it is an emergency, which is the only time this should
# fire. systemd's own default (60%/30s) would be closer to routine.
#
# Checked against practice rather than reasoned alone: systemd's default is
# 60%/30s; Fedora ships 50%/20s on user@.service, deliberately aggressive
# because a desktop is latency-sensitive; production guidance is to start
# near 50% and tune after observing real pressure. 80%/60s is more
# conservative than all three, which is correct for a BATCH fleet where a
# throttled worker finishing slowly beats a killed worker losing its work.
# Duration is NOT settable per-slice: ManagedOOMMemoryPressureDurationSec is
# not a valid key in systemd 255 (verified - systemd-analyze reports
# 'Unknown key name ... ignoring'). It is global only, as
# DefaultMemoryPressureDurationSec in /etc/systemd/oomd.conf.d/99-fleet.conf,
# repo-owned at systemd/system/oomd.conf.d/99-fleet.conf (fleet-ops#3971),
# set to 60s there: at 80% a 30s spike is survivable, a full sustained minute is not.
#
# Provenance (fleet-ops#71): the kill path is now PROVEN, not assumed.
# Bounded thrasher (MemoryHigh=128M, MemoryMax=1G, MemorySwapMax=0,
# RuntimeMaxSec=180) in oomd-drill.slice on 2026-08-26 01:24 IST. systemd
# journal at that minute, system-scope:
#   systemd-oomd[3099719]: Killed
#     /user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service/oomd.slice/oomd-drill.slice/oomd-drill-hog.service
#     due to memory pressure for /user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service
#     being 75.07% > 50.00% for > 1min with reclaim activity
# Service exited Result=oom-kill; ssh, the heartbeat, and intake survived.
# The 50.00% trip was Ubuntu's STOCK user@.service policy
# (10-oomd-user-service-defaults.conf) — NOT our 80% layer, which fires
# higher. Stock layer was not yet neutralized on 2026-08-26 01:24; it was
# neutralized at 01:34 via
# /etc/systemd/system/user@1000.service.d/50-no-distro-oomd-kill.conf
# (ManagedOOMMemoryPressure=auto). Both that drop-in AND
# /etc/systemd/system/user-1000.slice.d/50-ram-governor.conf are
# repo-owned by fleet-ops#71. See docs/ram-governor-tree.md for the
# full five-layer policy tree.
# Reproduce the drill after any oomd/kernel upgrade: bin/oomd-drill (issue #62).
#
# ManagedOOMSwap is deliberately left `auto` (off). 4.7 GiB of swap was in use
# at configuration time with ZERO pressure - swapped-out pages of workers idling
# on API calls are healthy here, and killing on swap usage would reap useful work.
#
# Scope: this slice only. sshd, tailscaled, the fleet heartbeat and the intake
# timers are NOT in it and stay `auto`, so oomd can never take the box's
# lifelines or the supervisor that would repair a reaped worker.
#
# Policy stack (outermost → innermost):
#   systemd/system/oomd.conf.d/99-fleet.conf → /etc/systemd/oomd.conf.d/99-fleet.conf
#                                                   60s pressure duration (fleet-ops#3971)
#   /etc/systemd/system/user-1000.slice.d/...         12 GiB cap + 80% kill (this issue)
#   user@1000.service                                 50% stock kill (now neutralized — this issue)
#   this slice (app-pi\x2dissue.slice)                80% kill (the LAST resort for fleet workers)
#   per-worker MemoryMax=4G (fleet-ops+0509, no MemoryHigh — fleet-ops#3930)  bound-and-local-OOM
#   MIN_FREE_RAM_MB=2500 launch floor                 prevent work starting
[Slice]
ManagedOOMMemoryPressure=kill
ManagedOOMMemoryPressureLimit=80%
